WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.832 --> 00:00:05.023
The IT team builds the new MES for the orders on the floor.

2
00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:08.323
Whoever builds it also keeps it running.

3
00:00:09.332 --> 00:00:11.735
You lead that IT project for the plant manager.

4
00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:15.692
The next version gets a screen with the rejected pieces,

5
00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:18.257
and it has to reach line 3 safely.

6
00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:22.612
We follow that one version through the four topics of the module.

7
00:00:23.302 --> 00:00:25.856
The details come later, in the lessons.

8
00:00:27.203 --> 00:00:30.338
The first topic is the pipeline and test automation.

9
00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:33.925
How does a change from the IT team reach the floor?

10
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:37.409
A pipeline is an automated conveyor.

11
00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:42.874
Every change runs through the same steps, from building to a test environment.

12
00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:48.415
Automated tests check every change again, without anyone having to click.

13
00:00:49.146 --> 00:00:51.457
If one fails, the line stops.

14
00:00:52.474 --> 00:00:55.261
For the pipeline and test automation we often

15
00:00:55.296 --> 00:00:57.885
use version control and a test pyramid.

16
00:00:59.232 --> 00:01:03.691
The second topic is the DORA metrics, from research into IT teams.

17
00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:06.820
How do you know whether the IT team delivers well?

18
00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:09.546
Two measures are about speed.

19
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.843
How often does a new version reach the floor,

20
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.467
and how long does a change take to get there?

21
00:01:16.478 --> 00:01:17.964
Two are about stability.

22
00:01:18.676 --> 00:01:22.949
How often does a new version go wrong, and how quickly are you back on track?

23
00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:26.129
The best teams score well on all four.

24
00:01:26.877 --> 00:01:28.978
So always look at all four together.

25
00:01:29.994 --> 00:01:33.802
With DORA we often use a dashboard and a value stream map.

26
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:38.563
The third topic is managing environments and releases.

27
00:01:39.277 --> 00:01:42.087
How does the new version reach line 3 safely?

28
00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:46.687
A new version passes development, test and acceptance.

29
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:51.726
There the team lead of line 3 checks the screen, because he knows the floor.

30
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.609
Only then comes production, the real environment with real orders.

31
00:01:57.608 --> 00:01:59.547
Installing is not yet releasing.

32
00:02:00.258 --> 00:02:03.114
The screen is already in the real environment,

33
00:02:03.254 --> 00:02:05.982
but the plant chooses when line 3 sees it.

34
00:02:07.045 --> 00:02:10.749
How many lines a fault hits is called the blast radius.

35
00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:12.625
You keep it small.

36
00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:16.120
So a new version goes to one line first.

37
00:02:17.162 --> 00:02:21.632
For managing releases we often use a feature flag and a rollback plan.

38
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:26.590
The fourth topic is IT service management and incidents.

39
00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:30.231
What do you do when the MES on the floor stops?

40
00:02:31.249 --> 00:02:35.765
If the MES stops, the operators report it to the service desk.

41
00:02:36.516 --> 00:02:40.963
With an incident one thing counts, getting back to work as fast as possible.

42
00:02:42.006 --> 00:02:45.455
If the same fault keeps coming back, we look for the cause.

43
00:02:46.188 --> 00:02:47.140
That is a problem.

44
00:02:48.137 --> 00:02:52.317
We agree with the plant how fast we respond and how fast we fix.

45
00:02:53.035 --> 00:02:55.961
A line that is down comes before an office printer.

46
00:02:56.997 --> 00:02:59.783
After a big outage we hold a post-mortem.

47
00:03:00.496 --> 00:03:04.258
We look for what in the system made the error possible, not who made it.

48
00:03:05.053 --> 00:03:06.156
That is blameless.

49
00:03:07.174 --> 00:03:11.505
For incidents we often use a priority matrix and five whys.

50
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:13.994
It all connects.

51
00:03:14.699 --> 00:03:18.170
The pipeline carries the change, DORA measures how it goes,

52
00:03:18.402 --> 00:03:22.674
the roll-out keeps it safe and incident management catches the rest.

53
00:03:23.735 --> 00:03:25.686
Speed and stability go together.

54
00:03:26.392 --> 00:03:31.291
Small changes are easier to test and to recover than one big version a quarter.

55
00:03:32.656 --> 00:03:34.316
This is the map of Projects.

56
00:03:35.024 --> 00:03:38.995
DevOps and delivery is the third station of IT and agile.

57
00:03:39.988 --> 00:03:43.819
DevOps and delivery has four topics, each with four levels.

58
00:03:44.837 --> 00:03:46.045
Start with the pipeline.

59
00:03:46.743 --> 00:03:50.749
At Basics level it is about what DevOps is, what DORA measures,

60
00:03:50.935 --> 00:03:52.909
environments and incidents.

61
00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:56.558
At Working knowledge you add the kinds of tests,

62
00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.669
honest measuring, feature flags and availability.

63
00:04:00.671 --> 00:04:02.668
At Advanced you add merging daily,

64
00:04:02.796 --> 00:04:06.534
reading reports, roll-out strategies and the outage timeline.

65
00:04:07.574 --> 00:04:11.127
At Expert you add delivering on the floor, steering with DORA,

66
00:04:11.208 --> 00:04:14.238
fixing forward and Lean in IT operations.

67
00:04:15.268 --> 00:04:19.332
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
